w wasn't that a bit of a red  herring to a point another phrase so the  fisheries i think we can sort out by the  way  uh i i never thought i would uh see a  stumbling block for a tory government to  be  the the right to dole out lots of state  aid  has there been have these talks from the  start phillip  been characterized by a certain  mutual mutual incomprehension  yes i and i think also a lack of trust  and i think that lack of trust has been  there um  all the way through but was exacerbated  of course by the uk internal market bill  happily now those clauses uh that  offended the eu and indeed other  international partners have now  have now been withdrawn but i think  there has been this mutual  incomprehension on the eu side what has  driven brexit  and from the uk side a misunderstanding  of how the eu operates and where  the deep concerns of the eu lie and  and that's just not been very helpful um  throughout all of this  and is perhaps a signal also of the way  in which  the uk through our membership um never  really got sort of integrated into  understanding the operation of the eu  uh in a way that's second nature to uh  to the the countries of are  more core to that enterprise if we were  to leave on  no deal would the in your view would  there then  quite quickly by that i mean sometime  next year maybe  after the summer would there be a move  then to try and reopen  the talks and do a free trade deal again  try and get that done  or would would relations actually  both sides may be so angry that  relations could end up in the freezer  for quite  some time yeah three quick things to say  about that one is  um that the eu and the uk will have to  talk about  stuff from here on forever whether we  like it or not  including fisheries common resource the  fish  don't respect the median line they don't  get spawned as a british or a french  fish  the stocks move around you have to  manage these resources in common  and there'll be loads of other stuff  where we have to talk second point  uh this relationship is too important  both on trade and on internal security  and on a bunch of other things  not to formalize the relationship at  some point  but third point i agree with you with  you when you say that the  risk is if we have no deal at the end of  this year tempers will be such and  political pride will be such  that neither side will be willing to be  the one that says oh  we need to get first we need to get back  round the table  so it could be some time before  negotiations resume to put the  relationship on a sound footing  when you stand back a little bit from  trying to work out whether we're going  to get a deal or  no deal and get away from the incredible  detail that goes in  to these talks uh whether you were for  remain  or whether you were for leave when you  look at uh  how close the eu in the uk how many  areas we will need to cooperate in the  2020s  and beyond our common interests as well  in or out  of the you know it's our biggest market  we have a common interest  in the eurozone doing well whether we're  in the eu or not  in defense and security and intelligence  and let me just standing up for  democracy  and human rights we have all so much in  common  we we are free traders you know we don't  want  tariffs if we can is it if we don't get  a deal  is it not really depressing that two  sides with so much in common  just couldn't do a bloody trade deal yes  and and actually a failure of statecraft  on both sides after four and a bit years  not to be able to manage that and let's  not even know the deal that's on the  table as you described it earlier it's  quite a thin  deal no deal is damaging it's  economically damaging  for both sides the application of  tariffs in terms of automotives  agri-food and so on even for fisheries  is very damaging  all the other agreements fall away like  on internal security  on research and all the rest of it even  australia  i know that people talk about aust